<p> Our goal is to explain how to create a <em>Math App</em> to be used in
Möbius.  Obviously, <em>Math Apps</em> can be used inside Maple.  The only
difference is how <em>Math Apps</em> are launched and how the arguments are
supplied to the <em>Math Apps</em>. </p>

<h3> Introduction </h3>

<p> Suppose that you want to build a <em>Math App</em> where, given a
function and an initial condition, students have to draw the cobweb
associated to the iteration of the function starting at the initial
condition.  The <em>Math App</em> will evaluate if the cobweb is
right or wrong based on a predetermined number of iterations and
tolerated accuracy. </p> 

<p> The question in Möbius is displayed below. </p>

<div class="center">
  <img src="Mobius_Math_App_1.png" style="width: 600px"
       alt="Screenshot of the question as it appears in Möbius"
       class="MobiusFig">
</div>

<p> To draw the cobweb, students click on the figure with the left
button of their mouse to enter in consecutive order the corners of the
cobweb.  The <em>Math App</em> draws the line between consecutive points
to produce the cobweb.  Students may use a text area to adjust the
coordinates of the last point that they have added to the figure.
Students may click on the button <em>Clear / Effacer</em> to start over
if they are not satisfy of their work.</p>

<p> A portion of the code to create the question in Möbius shown below. </p>

<div class="center">
  <img src="Mobius_Math_App_2.png" style="width: 600px"
       alt="Screenshot of a portion of the code in Möbius used to create the question"
       class="MobiusFig">
</div>

<p> The <em>Math App</em> component is added to the question using the
item <em>Möbius component</em> of the top menu.  When you add a
<em>Math App</em> to a question or when you click on an already
present <em>Math App</em>, you get the following display to select
the <em>Math App</em> and set the parameters that will be provided to
it. </p>

<div class="center">
  <img src="Mobius_Math_App_3.png" style="width: 600px"
       alt="Screenshot of the display used to add the Math App to the question"
       class="MobiusFig">
</div>

<ol style="list-style-type: decimal;">
<li> Like any component of a question in Möbius, you have to determine the
  weight (item <em>1<em> in the figure above) of the component
  associated to the <em>Math App</em> relative 
  to the other components of the question.</li>
<li> You can select the <em>Math App</em> by clicking on the icon
  (item <em>2</em> in the figure above) of a file at the end of the
  line <em>Worksheet File: ...</em> (item <em>3</em> in the figure
  above).  This is the item that you select if your <em>Math App</em>
  is located in your course <em>Content Repository</em>. </li>
<li> The <em>Worksheet Width</em> (item <em>4</em> in the figure
  above) is the width of the display for the <em>Math App</em>. </li>
<li> All the parameter values to be supplied to
  the <em>Math App</em> must be included in the section
  <em>Initialization Parameters</em> (item <em>5</em> in the figure
  above).  The name of a parameter is defined <em>without</em> the
  dollar sign in front of it is the case for the variables in Möbius.
  Only the parameters with a name that match the name of a parameter
  in the <em>Math App</em> are considered. </li>
<li> There are several ways to grade the cobweb drawn by the students.
  If your <em>Math App</em> handles the grading, then you must
  select <em>Grading Algorithm in Math App</em> (item <em>6</em> in
  the figure above).  This is the case for the <em>math App</em>
  described below. </li>
</ol>

<h3> Overview of the <em>Math App</em> </h3>

<p> Here is the display of the <em>Math App</em> in Maple. </p>

<div class="center">
  <img src="Mobius_Math_App_4.png" style="width: 600px"
       alt="Screenshot of the display of the Math App that is created in Maple"
       class="MobiusFig">
</div>

<p> The <em>Math App</em> is composed of the following components:</p>
<ol style="list-style-type: decimal;">
<li> a plot (named <font class="matlab">Plot0</font>),</li>
<li> a button <em> Clear / Effacer</em> (named
<font class="matlab">Button0</font>),</li>
<li> a button <em>Correction</em> (named <font class="matlab">Button1</font>)
with an associated label <em>Grade</em> (named
<font class="matlab">Label1</font>) and a text area
(named <font class="matlab">TextArea1</font>),</li>
<li> a button <em>Solution</em> (named
<font class="matlab">Button2</font>), and </li>
<li> a label <em>Coordinates</em> (named <font class="matlab">Label0</font>)
with an associated text area (named <font class="matlab">TextArea0</font>)
for for the coordinates of the last point drawn on the figure.</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>The purpose of each component is not hard to guess. </p>

<ol style="list-style-type: decimal;">
<li> The purpose of the button <em> Clear / Effacer</em> is clear
(no pun intended).  It is used by students who want to start over drawing
the cobweb.</li>
<li>Students who click on the button <em>Correction</em> have
their cobweb graded and their grade appears in the text area for the
grade.  This text area is not editable.  The
button <em>Correction</em>, the label <em>Grade</em> and the text 
associated to the grade should be hidden when the <em>Math App</em> is
used inside our question in Möbius.  You do not want the students to
check if they have the right solution before submitting their
assignment. </li>
<li> Students who click on the button <em>Solution</em> get the answer
of the question.  Again, this button should be hidden when the <em>Math
App</em> is used inside your question for obvious reasons.</li>
<li> Students may use the text area associated to the
button <em>Coordinates</em> to adjust the
coordinates of the latest point that the have added to the figure.</li>
</ol>

<p> There are a large choice of components that can be used to create
a <em>Math App</em>.  At this time, they are <em>Button</em>,
<em>Toggle Button</em>, <em>Combo Box</em>, <em>Check Box</em>,
<em>Radio Button</em>, <em>Text Area</em>, <em>Label</em>,
<em>List Box</em>, <em>Slider</em>, <em>Rotary Gauge</em>,
<em>Volume Gauge</em>, <em>Meter</em>, <em>Dial</em>, <em>Plot</em>,
<em>Mathematical Expression</em>, <em>Shortcut</em>, <em>Microphone</em>,
<em>Speaker</em>, <em>Data Table</em> and <em>Video Player</em>.  They
can be found in the left menu bar of the Maple display.</p>

<div class="center">
  <img src="Mobius_Math_App_5.png" style="width: 200px"
       alt="Screenshot of the left menu of the Maple display"
       class="MobiusFig">
</div>

<p>We will come back in the next section on adding components to create a
  <em>Math App</em>. </p>

<h3> Code of the <em>Math App</em> </h3>

<p> In the top menu of the Maple display, you select
<em>Edit &rarr; Starting Code</em> (items <em>7</em> and <em>8</em> in
the figure below). </p>

<div class="center">
  <img src="Mobius_Math_App_6.png" style="width: 250px"
       alt="Screenshot of the top menu of the Maple display"
       class="MobiusFig">
</div>

<p>to get the following display.</p>

<div class="center">
  <img src="Mobius_Math_App_7.png" style="width: 600px"
       alt="Screenshot of the window to enter the core code of the Math App"
       class="MobiusFig">
</div>

<p>Obviously, this display is empty when you access it for the first
time.  This is the location of the core code of the <em>Math App</em>. 
We have already written the code.  We explain in 
details all the elements of this code.</p> 

<ol style="list-style-type: upper-alpha;">
<li> <p> Since the functions 
<font class="matlab">SetProperty</font>
and <font class="matlab">GetProperty</font> are often used, it is wise
to define two macros to simplify the code.</p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
macro(SP = DocumentTools:-SetProperty);
macro(GP = DocumentTools:-GetProperty);
</font>
</pre>
</li>
<li> <p> The command <font class="matlab">Actions := module()</font>
indicates the beginning of the module that will be used by your
<em>Math App</em>.  The command <font class="matlab">export</font>
declares as globally accessible all the procedures of the module that
are used by the components of the <em>Math App</em>.</p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
Actions := module()

export Click, Refresh, Drag, EndDrag, DoPlot, Reset, Grade, InitParams, Check, Answer;
</font>
</pre>
</li>
<li> <p> There is a declaration of the variables that are local to
the module but can be used by all the procedures.  The arrays are
initialized by empty arrays.  </p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
local A, B, Err, Nbr, F, X0, MV, Pts:=Array(1..0), NbrGood :=0, GRAPH, SOL := Array(1..0), myskyblue := ColorTools:-Color([.196, .6, .8]);
</font>
</pre>
</li>
<li> <p>The procedure <font class="matlab">InitParams</font> is called by
Möbius to initialize the <em>Math App</em> when the question of the
assignment is read for the first time by the student.</p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
# The function InitParams() is called when run inside Mobius.
InitParams := proc( { a:=0, b:=1, err:=0.05, nbr:=3, f:= (1/2)*x + 1/4, x0 := 0.2, mv:= 0, flag1 := 0 } )
   local graph1, graph2, graph3, j, xn, yn;

   if (flag1 = 0)  then
      SP(Button1, visible, false);
      SP(Button2, visible, false);
      SP(Label1, visible, false);
      SP(TextArea1, visible, false);
   else
      SP(Button1, visible, true);
      SP(Button2, visible, true);
      SP(Label1, visible, true);
      SP(TextArea1, visible, true);
   end if;
   A := a;
   B := b;
   Err := err;
   Nbr := nbr;
   F := f;
   X0 := x0;
   if ( mv = 0 ) then
      MV := B;
   else
      MV := mv;
   end if;

   xn := X0;
   yn := 0;
   ArrayTools[Append](SOL, [xn,yn]);
   yn := evalf(subs(x=xn, F));
   ArrayTools[Append](SOL, [xn,yn]);
   for j from 1 to Nbr-1 do
      xn := yn;
      ArrayTools[Append](SOL, [xn,xn]);
      yn := evalf(subs(x=xn, F));
      ArrayTools[Append](SOL, [xn,yn]);
   end do;

   graph1 := plot([[A,A],[B,B]], color=myskyblue, gridlines=true, view=[A..B,0..MV]):
   graph2 := plot( F, x=A..B, color=black, view=[A..B,0..MV]):
   # graph3 is used only to ensure that the horizontal axis will be displayed.
   graph3 := plots[pointplot]([[A,0],[B,0]], symbol=point, color=black):
   GRAPH := plots[display]([graph1, graph2, graph3]);
   Reset();
end proc;
</font>
</pre>
<p> The initial parameters are listed below.
<ol style="list-style-type: decimal;">
<li> <font class="matlab">flag1</font> is set to 0 when the <em>Math App</em>
is used in a graded assignment to hide the button <em>Correction</em>
and its associated label and text area, and the button
<em>Solution</em> as explained above.</li>
<li> <font class="matlab">A</font> and <font class="matlab">B</font>
are the end points of the interval \([A,B]\), </li>
<li><font class="matlab">Err</font> is the tolerated error for the
locations of the points drawn by the students.</li>
<li><font class="matlab">Nbr</font> is the number of points that is
used to grade the students' answer starting with the initial
condition. </li>
<li><font class="matlab">F</font> is the function to be iterated.</li>
<li><font class="matlab">X0</font> is the initial condition.</li>
<li><font class="matlab">MV</font> is the maximum value
of <font class="matlab">F</font>. It is generally better to let the
code set this value.</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p> The variable <font class="matlab">SOL</font> is initialized with all
the points that form the corners of the cobweb starting with the
initial condition at <font class="matlab">(X0,0}</font>.</p>

<p> The plot <font class="matlab">GRAPH</font> that is displayed in the
question is composed of three sub-plots.  The plot
<font class="matlab">graph1</font> contains the diagonal
line \(y = x\).  The plot <font class="matlab">graph2</font> contains
the graph of the function <font class="matlab">F</font> between
<font class="matlab">A</font> and <font class="matlab">B</font>.  The
plot <font class="matlab">graph3</font> contains the \(x\)-axis.  This
third plot ensure that the horizontal axis is displayed an covers the
full range of the interval \([A,B]\).</p>
</li>
<li> The next procedure is <font class="matlab">Click</font>.  This is
the procedure that is called when a student clicks on the plot to draw
a point.</p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
Click := proc()
   local x, y;

   x := GP('Plot0', 'clickx');
   y := GP('Plot0', 'clicky');

   ArrayTools[Append](Pts, [x,y]);
   SP(TextArea0, value, [evalf[6](x),evalf[6](y)]);
   DoPlot();
end proc;
</font>
</pre>

<p>This procedure does three things.  It first gets the \(x\) and \(y\)
coordinates of the point that a student wants to draw.  It
adds these coordinates to the variable <font class="matlab">Pts</font>.
Finally, it draws in the figure <font class="matlab">Plot0</font> the
newest point and a straight line from the previous point to the new
point if needed.</p> 

<p>You have to attach this procedure to the plot
  <font class="matlab">Plot0</font> otherwise it does not know that it
  has to call the procedure <font class="matlab">Click</font>.  To do
  that, you go to the display of the <em>Math App</em> in Maple and
  click on the plot <font class="matlab">Plot0</font>.  You then get 
  the following menu on the right of the display in Maple.  </p>

<div class="center">
  <img src="Mobius_Math_App_8.png" style="width: 500px"
       alt="Screenshot of the menu that you get by clicking on the plot of the math app."
       class="MobiusFig">
</div>

<p>By clicking on the button <em>Edit Click Code</em> (item <em>9</em>
in the figure above), you get the following display where you can
enter the code to be executed when a student click on the plot. </p>

<div class="center">
  <img src="Mobius_Math_App_9.png" style="width: 400px"
       alt="Screenshot of the display to enter the code to be executed when the student click on the plot."
       class="MobiusFig">
</div>

<p> Initially, this section contains a default code like
<font class="matlab">with(DocumentTools): ...</font> or
<font class="matlab">Use DocumentTools in ...</font>.  That code must
be deleted because it will not work inside Möbius.  This two calls
work fine inside Maple but clash with some of the code used by Möbius.
For your <em>Math App</em>, it suffices to enter the line</p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
DocumentTools[Do](Actions:-Click() );
</font>
</pre>
<p>More code could be added but the variables defined in
the <em>Starting Code</em> are not accessible.</p>
</li>
<li> <p>The next two procedures are <font class="matlab">Drag</font>
and <font class="matlab">EndDrag</font></p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
Drag := proc()
   local x, y, N;

   x := GP('Plot0', 'endx');
   y := GP('Plot0', 'endy');
   N := numelems(Pts);
   Pts[N] := [x,y];
   SP(TextArea0, value, [evalf[6](x),evalf[6](y)]);
   DoPlot();
end proc;

EndDrag := proc()
   return NULL;
end proc;
</font>
</pre>
<p>Like the procedure <font class="matlab">Click</font>, the procedure
<font class="matlab">Drag</font> does three things.  It first gets the
new \(x\) and \(y\) coordinates of the last point drawn by the student
after the student has dragged it.  It modifies the variable
<font class="matlab">Pts</font> by replacing the old coordinates of
the last point drawn by the new coordinates.
Finally, it draws in the figure <font class="matlab">Plot0</font> the
newest point and redraws a straight line from the previous point to
the new point if necessary.</p> 

<p>The procedure <font class="matlab">EndDrag</font> does nothing.</p>

<p>As you did for the procedure <font class="matlab">Click</font>,
you must attach the procedures <font class="matlab">Drag</font> and
<font class="matlab">endDrag</font> to the plot using the items
<em>Edit Dragged Code</em> and <em>Edit Drag End Code</em>
of the right menu respectively (items <em>10</em> and <em>11</em>
respectively in the figure previous to the last one).  It suffices to
use the code </p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
DocumentTools[Do](Actions:-Drag() );
</font>
</pre>
<p>for the section under <em>Edit Dragged Code</em> and the code</p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
DocumentTools[Do](Actions:-endDrag() );
</font>
</pre>
<p>for the section under <em>Edit Drag End Code</em>.</p>

<p> Since the procedure <font class="matlab">ednDrag</font> does not
do anything, you could have simply inserted a comment in the section under
<em>Edit Drag End Code</em> and have completely ignored the procedure
<font class="matlab">endDrag</font>.  That is what is done for the
section associated to <em>Edit Hover Code</em>
(item <em>12</em> in the figure previous to the last one)
as illustrate below.</p>

<div class="center">
  <img src="Mobius_Math_App_10.png" style="width: 400px"
       alt="Screenshot of the display to enter the code to be executed when the student move the mouse over the plot."
       class="MobiusFig">
</div>
</li>
<li> <p> The procedure <font class="matlab">Refresh</font> is attached
to the text area associated to the label <em>Coordinates</em>.  Student
can modify the coordinates of the last point that they have drawn by
writing the coordinates that they want this point to have in the text area.
The coordinates must be in the format <em>[x,y]</em> (using square
brackets).</p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
Refresh := proc()
   local x, y, N, coord;
	
   coord := parse(GP('TextArea0','value'));
   x := coord[1];
   y := coord[2];

   if ( is(x < A) or is(x > B) ) then 
      return NULL;
   end if;

   N := numelems(Pts);
   Pts[N] := [x,y];
   DoPlot();
end proc;
</font>
</pre>
<p> The procedure does four things.  It first gets the
new \(x\) and \(y\) coordinates submitted by the student.  There is a
minimal test to make sure that the \(x\) coordinate is at least in the
interval \([A,B]\).  It modifies the variable
<font class="matlab">Pts</font> by replacing the old coordinates of
the last point drawn by the new coordinates.
Finally, it draws in the figure <font class="matlab">Plot0</font> the
newest point and redraws a straight line from the previous point to
the new point if necessary.</p> 

<p>To attach this procedure to the text area for
the label <em>Coordinates</em>, you go to the display
of the <em>Math App</em> in Maple and click on the text area
associated to the label <em>Coordinates</em>.  You get
the following menu on the right of the display in Maple.  </p>

<div class="center">
  <img src="Mobius_Math_App_11.png" style="width: 250px"
       alt="Screenshot of the menu that you get by clicking on the text area associated to the Coordinates label of the math app."
       class="MobiusFig">
</div>

<p>By clicking on the button <em>Edit Content Change Code</em>
  (item <em>13</em> in the figure above), you get the
following display where you can enter the code to be executed when a
student click on the plot. </p>

<div class="center">
  <img src="Mobius_Math_App_12.png" style="width: 300px"
       alt="Screenshot of the display to enter the code for the text area associated to the Coordinates label of the math app."
       class="MobiusFig">
</div>

<p> In the present case, the code used is </p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
DocumentTools[Do](Actions:-Refresh() );
</font>
</pre>
</li>
<li> The procedure <font class="matlab">DoPlot</font> is not directly
attached to a component of the <em>Math App</em> but it is
indirectly called by the procedures that are attached to components of
the <em>Math App</em>.  This procedure draws the cobweb using the
points provided by the student.  These points mark the corners of the
student's cobweb. </p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
DoPlot := proc()
   local vertices, edges;

   vertices := plots[pointplot](Pts, symbol=solidcircle, symbolsize=10, color=blue):
   if ( numelems(Pts) > 1 ) then
      edges := plot(Pts, color=blue);
      SP(Plot0, value, plots[display]([GRAPH, vertices, edges]));
   else
      SP(Plot0, value, plots[display]([GRAPH, vertices]));
   end if;
end proc;
</font>
</pre>

<p>As mentioned before, this procedures not only draw the points
provided by the student but also draws the lines connecting
consecutive points.</p>
</li>
<li> <p>The procedure <font class="matlab">Reset</font> is attached to
the button <em>Clear / Effacer</em>.  It erases the cobweb drawn by
the student and resets the figure to its initial state.  This
procedure also clears the text areas associated to the
buttons <em>Coordinates</em> and <em>Grade</em> if present.  </p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
Reset := proc()
   Pts := Array(1..0);
   NbrGood := 0;
   SP(Plot0, value, GRAPH);
   SP(TextArea0, value, "");
   SP(TextArea1, value, "");
end proc;
</font>
</pre>
<p>As you have seen before, to attach this procedure to the button
<em>Clear</em>, you go to the display of the <em>Math App</em>
in Maple and click on the button to get the right menu in the Maple
display to edit this button.  By clicking on the button
<em>Edit Click Code</em>
in the right menu, you get the display to enter the code to be executed
when a student clicks on this button.  The code used is </p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
DocumentTools[Do](Actions:-Reset() );
</font>
</pre>
</li>
<li> <p> When the <em>Math App</em> is used for practice, the instructor
may give access to the button <em>Solution</em> that students may
use to request the solution.  The following procedure is attached
this button. </p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
Answer := proc()
   Reset();
   Pts := SOL;
   DoPlot();
end proc;
</font>
</pre>
<p> The procedure does three things.  It first clears the figure to
reset it to its initial state.   It fills the array
<font class="matlab">Pts</font> used to draw the cobweb with the exact
coordinates of the points forming the corners of the cobweb.  Finally,
it draws the cobweb.</p>

<p>The procedure to attach this code to the button
<em>Solution</em> is identical to the procedure used to attach the
procedure <font class="matlab">Clear</font> to its button.  The only
difference is that the code inserted in the section under
<em>Edit Click Code</em> is</p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
DocumentTools[Do](Actions:-Answer() );
</font>
</pre>
</li>
<li> <p>The procedure <font class="matlab">Check</font> determines
which of the points selected by the student are correct.  It also
redraw the cobweb submitted by the student where the good elements of
the cobweb appear in green and the bad ones are in red.  This
procedure is called by the procedure <font class="matlab">Grade</font>
that comes next. </p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
Check := proc()
   local N1, N2, j, J:=0, k, pt, d, indices, goodEdges:=Array(1..0), badEdges:=Array(1..0), goodPts:=Array(1..0), badPts:=Array(1..0), badP, goodP;

   N1 := 2*Nbr;
   indices := Array(1..N1);
   N2 := numelems(Pts);
   NbrGood := 0;

   for k from 1 to N1 do
      if ( k <= N2 ) then
         pt := Pts[k];
         d := sqrt((SOL[k][1]-pt[1])^2 + (SOL[k][2]-pt[2])^2);
         if ( is(d < Err) ) then
            NbrGood := NbrGood + 1;
            indices[k] := 1;
         end if;
      else
         break;
      end if;
   end do;

   for j from 1 to N1 do
      if ( j <= N2 ) then
         if ( indices[j] = 1 ) then
            ArrayTools[Append](goodPts, Pts[j]);
            if ( j > 1 ) then
               if ( J = 1 ) then
                  ArrayTools[Append](goodEdges, plot([Pts[j-1],Pts[j]], color=green));
               else
                  ArrayTools[Append](badEdges, plot([Pts[j-1],Pts[j]], color=red));
               end if;
            end if;
            J := 1;
         else
            ArrayTools[Append](badPts, Pts[j]);
            if ( j > 1 ) then
               ArrayTools[Append](badEdges, plot([Pts[j-1],Pts[j]], color=red));
            end if;
            J := 0;
         end if;
      else
	 break;
         # ArrayTools[Append](badPts, SOL[j]);
         # if ( j-1 = N2 ) then
         #    ArrayTools[Append](badEdges, plot([Pts[N2],SOL[j]], color=red));
         # else
         #    ArrayTools[Append](badEdges, plot([SOL[j-1],SOL[j]], color=red));
         # end if;
      end if;
   end do;
	
   badP := plots[pointplot](badPts,symbol= diagonalcross, symbolsize=15, color=red);
   goodP := plots[pointplot](goodPts,symbol= solidcircle, symbolsize=10, color=green);
   SP(Plot0, value, plots[display]([GRAPH, goodP, badP, seq(goodEdges[i],i=1..numelems(goodEdges)),seq(badEdges[i],i=1..numelems(badEdges)) ]));
end proc;
</font>
</pre>
<p> The first loop count the number of correct points and track their
location in the array <font class="matlab">Pts</font>.  A point is
considered to be correct if its Euclidean distance to the right
location is less than the tolerated error.  The second loop determines
the straight lines that are correct (those linking two consecutive
correct points) and those that are not correct.  Finally, the entire
plot is redraw with the student cobweb where the good elements of the
cobweb are in green and the bad ones are in red.</p>

<p>The section of the code that has been commented out could be used
to get a complete correction based on the complete expected cobweb;
namely, not only limited to the points that the students has
provided. </p>
</li>
<li> <p>The procedure <font class="matlab">Grade</font> is the
procedure used by Möbius to mark the question when the assignment is
submitted.  It is also called by <em>How did I do?</em> in Möbius.
Moreover, it is also attached to the button <em>Correction</em> when
students are allowed to use this button in a practice assignment.</p>
<pre class="matlab">
<font class="matlab">
Grade := proc()
   local Nbr2;

   Check();
   Nbr2 := 2*Nbr;

   if ( NbrGood > 3*Nbr/2 ) then
      SP(TextArea1, value, evalf(NbrGood/Nbr2));
      return evalf(NbrGood/Nbr2);
   else
      SP(TextArea1, value, 0);
      return 0;
   end if;
end proc;
</font>
</pre>
<p>A grade other than 0 is given when at least 3/4 of the points drawn
by the student are right.  The grade is displayed in the text area
associated to the button <em>Correction</em>.  The name of this text
area is <font class="matlab">TextArea1</font>.  The grade is also
returned by this function.  This is the value used by Möbius. </p>
</li>
